   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## Summary\n\n| Group | Order | Realised by |\n|---|---|---|\n| $C_n$ | $n$ | a pinwheel; rotations about one axis |\n| $D_n$ | $2n$ | a rosette; $n$-gon with mirrors |\n| $T \\cong A_4$ | $12$ | tetrahedron |\n| $O \\cong S_4$ | $24$ | cube, octahedron |\n| $I \\cong A_5$ | $60$ | dodecahedron, icosahedron |\n\nEverything came from one equation,\n\n$$2 - \\frac{2}{N} = \\sum_{i=1}^k\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{n_i}\\right),$$\n\nobtained by counting the fixed points of a finite group acting on a sphere. Two orbits give the cyclic groups; three give the dihedral family plus exactly three exceptions. Those three exceptions are the Platonic solids, and there is no sixth solid because there is no fourth solution.\n\nAdding translations changes the answer completely: the crystallographic restriction $2\\cos(2\\pi/n) \\in \\mathbb Z$ leaves only $n \\in \\{1,2,3,4,6\\}$, from which the 7 frieze and 17 wallpaper groups follow.\n\n## The pattern to notice\n\nWe computed with a group but the answer was about a **sphere**. The orbit-counting equation is a disguised Euler characteristic: writing the pole orbits as vertices, edges and faces of a tessellation of $S^2$ recovers $V - E + F = 2$, and the quantity $\\sum(1/n_i) - 1 = 2/N$ is positive precisely because the sphere has positive curvature.\n\nChange the sign and the same equation classifies something else entirely:\n\n| $\\sum 1/n_i$ | Geometry | Groups |\n|---|---|---|\n| $> 1$ | spherical | the finite groups above |\n| $= 1$ | Euclidean | the Euclidean triangle groups $(2,3,6), (2,4,4), (3,3,3)$ |\n| $< 1$ | hyperbolic | infinitely many triangle groups |\n\nThat is the same trichotomy that will organise Stage 2, where the three geometries appear in their own right, and it returns for the last time as the sign of the curvature in Gauss–Bonnet (3.08). One equation, read three ways.\n\n## Next\n\n**1.05 — Affine Geometry and Barycentric Coordinates** takes the first step of the Erlangen ladder: drop the requirement that lengths be preserved, keep only that lines go to lines and parallels stay parallel. Distances and angles become meaningless; ratios along a line survive, and with them the centroid, Ceva's theorem and Menelaus' theorem.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## References\n\n- Armstrong, M.A. (1988) *Groups and Symmetry*. New York: Springer. Chapters 15–19 give the pole-counting classification of the finite subgroups of $SO(3)$ in the form used here.\n- Conway, J.H., Burgiel, H. and Goodman-Strauss, C. (2008) *The Symmetries of Things*. Wellesley, MA: A K Peters. The modern orbifold account, which makes the \"one equation, read three ways\" trichotomy at the end of this notebook into a systematic method.\n- Coxeter, H.S.M. (1973) *Regular Polytopes*. 3rd edn. New York: Dover. The classical source for the Platonic solids and their groups.\n- Euclid, *Elements*, Book XIII. The final propositions construct the five regular solids and prove that there are no others.\n\nOn the crystallographic restriction and quasicrystals: Shechtman, D. *et al.* (1984) 'Metallic phase with long-range orientational order and no translational symmetry', *Physical Review Letters*, 53(20), pp. 1951–1953.\n- ★ Martin, G.E. (1982) *Transformation Geometry: An Introduction to Symmetry*. Undergraduate Texts in Mathematics. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-1-4612-5680-9. Chapters 11–14 for the frieze and wallpaper classifications.\n- ★ Hartshorne, R. (2000) *Geometry: Euclid and Beyond*. Undergraduate Texts in Mathematics. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-0-387-22676-7. Chapter 8 covers the Platonic solids from the axiomatic side, and §47 treats their symmetry groups directly — the synthetic counterpart to the pole-counting proof given here.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = in the local indexed library (`maths` collection); the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell in this notebook was executed and its output is the real result; the prose is written from knowledge and checked against these sources where they cover the material.*",
   "metadata": {}
  }
 ]
}